1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
Why Domain Matters, part 3: Payments. The record says payment failed; the customer’s account was debited.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:09,800
Part 3 follows one evening’s payments through the people who see them differently.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:16,200
The same synthetic world: Store 014 in Jayanagar, now with every one of its 30,077 June bills paid at the tills.

4
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:23,600
One basket in every record that holds it. The till says the UPI payment failed; the provider’s report says success, 87 seconds later.

5
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:30,000
In the 18:00 and 19:00 hours the bank credited 17 UPI payments that the tills had marked failed.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,400
Payment success four ways: 61% at the till, 82% at the bank, 100% of bills paid, 87% of customers charged once.

7
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:42,800
The demo replays June with the same customers, baskets and banks. Change what the till does, or how the back office matches.

8
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:49,200
The tills give up on 28 UPI payments in 90 minutes. Each time, the cashier asks for another way to pay.

9
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:54,600
Payments operations sees approvals at 69% and 67% for two hours, normal by eight. Incident closed.

10
00:00:54,600 --> 00:01:01,000
Reconciliation sees 17 credits with no approved payment behind them. They sit in suspense until someone calls.

11
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,400
Support takes 23 calls from customers charged twice: a screenshot, a check, and about five days.

12
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:13,800
What actually happened: 15 customers paid twice in 90 minutes, 17 extra charges, ₹7,826 in all.

13
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:20,200
Wait for the status check at 90 seconds: double charges in June fall from 24 to 9, and the tills spend 99 minutes taking payment, not 77.

14
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:26,600
Put the bill ID on every UPI request: double charges still happen, but the median refund falls from 4.9 days to 0.7.

15
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:34,000
The records behind it. Nothing says “charged twice”. Download them and find the customers yourself.

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,400
The post ends with a learning path: how UPI moves money, RBI’s rules for failed payments, reconciliation, and practice on June’s records.

17
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:45,900
Every record was right about what it saw. Only the customer saw both debits.
